  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nRAP h5 analysis this morning shows a closed high centered near Lake \nSuperior, with its associated surface high pressure centered over \nQuebec. This surface high will remain parked over the Great \nLakes/eastern Canada through early next week. This surface pattern \nwill result in southeast winds continuing until a front passes \nMonday night or Tuesday next week. This will also setup a west to \neast gradient in moisture, with dewpoints and precipitation chances \nhighest in western MN and lowest in western WI until that more \ndefined fropa early next week.\n\nFor today, those dry southeast winds will rule the day, with the \ndeeper moisture plume and associated precipitation remaining in the \nDakotas. That moisture plume will lay into western MN overnight. \nBiggest change we've seen in the models over the last 24 hours is a \nslowing of the arrival of this deeper moisture and precipitation \nchances until after midnight tonight, even in western MN. \nEventually, we expect widespread showers to develop within the LLJ \nlate tonight, with an embedded storm or two likely. This area of \nrain will then fall apart during the day on Thursday as it runs into \nthe dry air associated with the high over the Great Lakes. Friday \nwill give a cloudy break from the rain, before we kind of have a \nrepeat of tonight into Thursday Friday night into Saturday. Once \nagain, we'll see a batch of showers with an embedded storm or two \ndevelop within an enhanced LLJ. Saturday won't be a washout, but the \ncloud cover and showers will keep temperatures more in the low 60s. \nLike tonight, high pressure in the Great Lakes results in lower \nprecip chances as you get into WI. For Sunday, it's back to our \ncool, cloudy, and dry weather as the enhanced h85 flow that drives \nthe Saturday rain falls apart.\n\nEarly next week, we are starting to see models come around on Monday \nnight/Tuesday for a boundary passage. The strength of that system \nvaries quite a bit, but the general idea of another uptick in \nprecipitation chances Monday night into Tuesday exists. It does look \nto get a bit warmer/more humid ahead of this boundary on Monday, so \nthere could be small chance of a stronger storm or two, but for the \nmost part, this just looks to continue the train of quarter to half \ninch rain events spaced a couple of days apart. The one big \ndifference though between this rain chance and what we see Thursday \nand Saturday as that we'll actually see a wind shift over to the \nnorthwest behind this rain chance as the blocking high over eastern \nCanada starts to break down.",
